WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.430
 

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:02.920
INTERVIEWER: Hello.

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:06.820
I'm Bill Lattanzi, and today
is April 27, 2012.

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:08.710
We are speaking with
J. Meejin Yoon.

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:12.490
She's an architect, designer,
and artist, the founder of MY

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:16.250
Studio, and co-founder of
Howeler and Yoon Architecture.

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:19.830
At MIT, Miss Yoon is associate
professor and director of the

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:21.810
undergraduate program
in architecture.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:23.480
Welcome, Meejin Yoon.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:24.800
YOON: Thank you.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:28.510
INTERVIEWER: The MIT community
probably best knows your work

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:31.620
from the FAST Festival last
year as part of the 150

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:36.440
celebration, when you, it's fair
to say, dressed the MIT

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:38.040
campus with art.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.413
Can you describe how that came
about and how you felt about

00:00:40.413 --> 00:00:41.990
how it went?

00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:44.960
YOON: Well, I think the FAST
Festival was an amazing

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:51.520
opportunity to bring together
different disciplines around

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:53.660
the arts at MIT.

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:56.780
The notion of having a Festival
for Art, Science, and

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:59.420
Technology I thought
was fantastic.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:04.150
And Todd Machover brought me on
board to curate a series of

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:09.270
installations by students and
faculty all around campus.

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:14.520
And the idea was to curate it
in such a way that over the

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:19.260
course of a semester these
installations would pop up in

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:25.720
an unexpected spaces, first in
kind of interior niches and

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:31.080
circulatory areas, to create a
kind of sense of, well, what

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:36.360
is that, and transform the space
for an instance, and

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:43.570
then culminate in a series of
more I would say strategically

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:46.500
placed installations that
highlighted the

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:50.090
river axis at MIT.

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:54.300
So the initial interior projects
from the winter

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:58.180
months happened along the
Infinite Corridor axis.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:01.330
And then the spring/summer
installations happened along

00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:05.090
the Memorial Drive, right
along the river.

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:08.150
And it was a way to kind of
gesture to Boston and

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:12.150
Cambridge, like this is also
our front door, even though

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:15.890
the kind of internal community
really uses the Infinite

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:18.790
Corridor kind of circulation.

00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:20.857
INTERVIEWER: It was a way of
expanding out people's sense

00:02:20.857 --> 00:02:21.060
of the campus?

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:21.330
YOON: Yes.

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:25.370
It was a way of sort of turning
MIT inside out for a

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:27.620
brief, brief moment
to the public.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:29.410
INTERVIEWER: And I think that
this wasn't just faculty work.

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:30.350
This was student work as well?

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:31.420
YOON: Yes, absolutely.

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:35.090
It was probably 50 percent
student work, maybe more than

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:38.620
50 percent student work and
50 percent faculty work.

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:42.550
And we really did try to
encourage collaborations

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:46.840
between theaters students and
architecture students and

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:50.020
Media Lab students for
those projects.

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:51.480
INTERVIEWER: Can you give me
an example of one of the

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:54.680
student or interactive projects
that were developed?

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:54.840
YOON: Sure.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:03:00.630
There was a project right off
the Infinite Corridor that was

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:06.380
done by a PhD computation
student, two PhD computation

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:10.340
students who collaborated with
some of the Media Lab

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:12.540
electronics engineers.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:16.160
And they created a kind of
interactive wall that was all

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:17.430
magnetized.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:23.620
And so the poles on the magnets
created different

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:27.880
kinetic movement on
the installation.

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:28.850
Yes.

00:03:28.850 --> 00:03:30.450
INTERVIEWER: So as I walked
by, it would move?

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:34.340
 

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:38.310
YOON: It wasn't responding to
necessarily your movement, but

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:40.730
you were moving the installation
and it was

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:44.000
triggering different lights
as you did that.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:44.620
INTERVIEWER: So here's
a question.

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:46.520
I often hear artists talk about,
this will make me think

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:47.410
what's happening here?

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:51.070
This will make me think afresh
about my environment.

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:52.340
Can you say more about that?

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:53.340
Why is that a good thing?

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:53.920
What's your aim?

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:54.310
YOON: Well, okay.

00:03:54.310 --> 00:03:57.870
So, you know, one of the student
installations, it was

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:59.190
called Dis(Course) 4.

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:01.070
They're Course 4 students.

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:04.760
And what they did was in one of
the four-story stairwells,

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:07.160
they hung a giant--

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:09.770
I can only describe it
as a chandelier.

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:13.130
But it was basically a modular
piece that was made out of

00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:17.350
different units that could
basically aggregate in a way

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:20.910
that tapered in and then
back out again in

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:23.120
that four-story stairwell.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:26.580
And what's interesting is we're
above or next to-- or

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:29.000
we, meaning architecture,
is next to mechanical

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:29.680
engineering.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:31.500
And then there's another
department the

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:33.350
floor below, et cetera.

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:37.580
But when this installation
happened, people I think went

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:40.690
out of their way to actually
circulate on this stairwell as

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:42.040
opposed to going--

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:45.630
I know I did, because it was
such a beautiful installation.

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:50.240
And you would see people kind of
obliquely one floor below.

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:52.170
And it did kind of foster--

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:57.450
because it was so unfamiliar,
by basically defamiliarizing

00:04:57.450 --> 00:05:02.430
one's environmental contacts,
it creates discussion and

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:08.060
questioning and asking, oh, who
is one floor below us in

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:09.440
mechanical engineering?

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:10.690
INTERVIEWER: That's great.

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:12.900
 

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:16.330
Partly I think on the basis of
the success of FAST-- it was

00:05:16.330 --> 00:05:19.510
tremendously enjoyed by the
community and surveys said

00:05:19.510 --> 00:05:22.075
that we should do more
of this at MIT--

00:05:22.075 --> 00:05:25.320
the University just announced
a new program, CAST, or the

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:28.950
Center for Art, Science,
and Technology.

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:31.570
What's art doing in the mix
here at MIT anyway?

00:05:31.570 --> 00:05:35.360
Aren't artists as far apart from
engineers as you can get?

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:35.600
YOON: I don't know.

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:39.300
I think if you look at the MIT
community, the artists here,

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:43.300
their work is inseparable from
science and technology.

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:47.570
And I do think there are certain
artists that might not

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:49.800
intersect with science
and technology.

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:53.910
But in the sense of the
quintessential Leonardo da

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:57.360
Vincis of the world, art,
science, and technology were

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:02.710
always intertwined, an
architect, an inventor, an

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:04.560
astronomer.

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:08.930
And I think that notion still
exists today in many people.

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:13.960
And I'm always amazed to find
engineers who are amazingly

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:17.640
creative or mathematicians who
are amazingly creative, and

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:22.450
then artists or architects who
are incredibly rigorous about

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:25.190
their design research.

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:27.860
So I don't think art, science,
and technology--

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:32.200
to me, they seem much more
seamless than I think maybe

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:37.140
the way our educational
kind of context--

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:42.290
not MIT's educational context,
but we grow up and think, oh,

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:47.010
you either go to a humanities
school or you go to a school

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:48.940
of science and technology.

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:53.390
You're either an artsy, literary
type of person or a

00:06:53.390 --> 00:06:55.620
science/math person.

00:06:55.620 --> 00:07:01.630
But especially when you're at
MIT, you find so many amazing

00:07:01.630 --> 00:07:05.760
students in math and science
are brilliant musicians or

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:08.710
artists or designers.

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:14.340
So I think this place makes
you more aware of the

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:16.990
seamlessness between art,
science, and technology.

00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:23.660
And if you talk to faculty,
I'm always amazed that the

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:29.510
lines are so crisscrossing
across the disciplines that

00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:30.630
it's very hard.

00:07:30.630 --> 00:07:33.055
You meet someone, and
they're like, oh, I

00:07:33.055 --> 00:07:34.850
do structural origami.

00:07:34.850 --> 00:07:37.070
And then you're like, huh, well,
that has so much to do

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:40.460
with structures in architecture
and design.

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:42.350
But they're looking
at it from math.

00:07:42.350 --> 00:07:49.110
Or a biologist who's looking at
how certain components come

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:51.460
together, we would think of them
as components, but he's,

00:07:51.460 --> 00:07:56.560
of course, thinking of them as
cellular aggregation, and how

00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:01.420
similar we are thinking about
building module aggregation.

00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:03.930
There's so much to learn
from each other.

00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:06.080
But if we're kind of separated
into these

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:07.680
silos that never happen.

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:14.080
So what was amazing about FAST
was its ability to foreground

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:16.100
art, science, and technology.

00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:19.530
I think MIT is a place where no
one questions the science

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:21.680
and technology.

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:25.680
But this is a place with
amazing, creative arts and

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:26.630
humanities.

00:08:26.630 --> 00:08:30.360
And the opportunity to really
bring that to the forefront

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:35.570
for the 150th celebration was,
I think, a great, great

00:08:35.570 --> 00:08:39.809
opportunity for those of us in
those fields, but also, I

00:08:39.809 --> 00:08:50.210
think, MIT became more aware
of the kind of potential in

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:55.070
the arts as it relates to the
Institute in general.

00:08:55.070 --> 00:08:56.880
INTERVIEWER: Showing the
community to itself or a side

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:58.430
of itself it wasn't aware of?

00:08:58.430 --> 00:08:59.590
YOON: Yes, absolutely.

00:08:59.590 --> 00:09:05.780
I mean, the number of people
that FAST Festival drew from

00:09:05.780 --> 00:09:11.050
the general Cambridge/Boston
community, I think it was a

00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:14.950
way of making the campus
more accessible

00:09:14.950 --> 00:09:18.700
to a general public.

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:23.380
In every installation, there
was research and technology

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:26.700
involved from digital
fabrication to rethinking

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:31.840
modular aggregation to new
materials through energy

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:35.750
production with the solar
rockers by Sheila Kennedy.

00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:39.440
I think people became aware
that, oh, there is science and

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:42.920
technology, but it can be
beautiful and change our

00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:48.330
environment and be pleasurable
and be playful as well.

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:49.790
INTERVIEWER: That's great.

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:52.660
There's a quote we
found that--

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:55.140
what's been called, there's a
kind of chain reaction that

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:56.690
happens between the
arts, science, and

00:09:56.690 --> 00:09:57.750
technology at MIT.

00:09:57.750 --> 00:09:59.650
Is this the kind of thing
you're talking about?

00:09:59.650 --> 00:10:00.070
YOON: Yes.

00:10:00.070 --> 00:10:00.830
Absolutely.

00:10:00.830 --> 00:10:04.840
I mean, I think the school, like
the Media Lab is probably

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:10.310
one of the let's say buildings
or envelopes that

00:10:10.310 --> 00:10:14.120
has the most variables.

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:17.840
There are artists there because
the Art, Culture,

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:20.380
Technology program
is based there.

00:10:20.380 --> 00:10:23.580
There are designers and
architects in terms of

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:26.710
students and faculty
in the Media Lab.

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:29.630
But there are also scientists,
physicists,

00:10:29.630 --> 00:10:31.370
engineers, et cetera.

00:10:31.370 --> 00:10:37.420
And I think you can sense, in
the amazing production that

00:10:37.420 --> 00:10:46.080
place generates, just
a creative energy.

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:48.750
And I think that creative energy
comes from the synergy

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:50.700
of multiple disciplines.

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:55.100
I think you need to bump up
against people from other

00:10:55.100 --> 00:10:58.960
disciplines in order to
re-question not only how you

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:02.270
think about your own discipline,
but, oh, in fact,

00:11:02.270 --> 00:11:07.790
aren't we much more connected
than we think in our desire to

00:11:07.790 --> 00:11:12.610
change the world but through
different avenues.

00:11:12.610 --> 00:11:13.990
INTERVIEWER: I was looking at
one of your talks and you

00:11:13.990 --> 00:11:17.160
talked about art, science, and
technology being something

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:20.530
when you put them together you
get a whole that's more than

00:11:20.530 --> 00:11:21.990
just plus signs between them.

00:11:21.990 --> 00:11:22.430
YOON: Yes.

00:11:22.430 --> 00:11:23.170
Absolutely.

00:11:23.170 --> 00:11:23.390
INTERVIEWER: Yes.

00:11:23.390 --> 00:11:24.490
Can you say a little more?

00:11:24.490 --> 00:11:27.790
I mean, you have been, but--

00:11:27.790 --> 00:11:28.130
YOON: Yes.

00:11:28.130 --> 00:11:31.120
I mean, I think that when there
is synergy between three

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:34.320
things you get much more than
the sum of its parts.

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:42.150
I think that the much more is
you get something unexpected.

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:48.600
I think typically you can
guess what this kind of

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:50.480
research might lead to,
what this kind of

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:51.610
research might lead to.

00:11:51.610 --> 00:11:58.080
But recently one of our alums,
Skylar Tibbits, who's one of

00:11:58.080 --> 00:11:59.690
the TED fellows this year, is

00:11:59.690 --> 00:12:01.850
collaborating with a biologist.

00:12:01.850 --> 00:12:03.890
And they're working on--

00:12:03.890 --> 00:12:10.820
basically, the biologist is
working on an area researching

00:12:10.820 --> 00:12:16.270
how cells know what
can combine and

00:12:16.270 --> 00:12:17.640
what can not combine.

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:17.960
Sorry.

00:12:17.960 --> 00:12:23.600
That's my very low, low
biology-speak of that idea.

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:27.780
And Skylar is also looking at
it by just using mechanical

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:31.940
energy, so like shaking a tube
of different modules, they

00:12:31.940 --> 00:12:35.670
aggregate in precise ways
because the modules are

00:12:35.670 --> 00:12:38.190
designed so they only go
together one way and not

00:12:38.190 --> 00:12:39.320
another way.

00:12:39.320 --> 00:12:42.130
And that he learned through
biology, through this

00:12:42.130 --> 00:12:43.540
collaboration.

00:12:43.540 --> 00:12:46.670
And I think you put two people
in two different disciplines

00:12:46.670 --> 00:12:49.990
together and you ask them, maybe
you should have coffee

00:12:49.990 --> 00:12:52.570
or a discussion and
collaborate.

00:12:52.570 --> 00:12:54.140
You can't force collaboration.

00:12:54.140 --> 00:12:56.690
But I think you never know
what's going to come out of

00:12:56.690 --> 00:13:01.790
that crash of ideas.

00:13:01.790 --> 00:13:02.190
INTERVIEWER: That's great.

00:13:02.190 --> 00:13:04.600
And there's a lot of that going
on at MIT, it seems.

00:13:04.600 --> 00:13:05.810
It's encouraging.

00:13:05.810 --> 00:13:07.035
I wanted to back up
for a minute.

00:13:07.035 --> 00:13:08.600
We'll come back to MIT.

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:10.810
I want to back up for a minute
and talk about what brought

00:13:10.810 --> 00:13:12.520
you to this point.

00:13:12.520 --> 00:13:14.880
But go all the way back and,
without telling your whole

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:18.400
life story, I can supply from
my research that you

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:19.250
were born in Seoul?

00:13:19.250 --> 00:13:19.740
YOON: Yes.

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:21.210
I was born in Seoul, Korea.

00:13:21.210 --> 00:13:24.440
But I grew up mostly in
the United States.

00:13:24.440 --> 00:13:27.945
I had a very traditional
architectural education.

00:13:27.945 --> 00:13:31.330
I have a bachelor of
architecture from Cornell, and

00:13:31.330 --> 00:13:34.940
then a master of architecture
and urban design from Harvard.

00:13:34.940 --> 00:13:38.850
But when I graduated, I did
a Fulbright in Korea.

00:13:38.850 --> 00:13:42.840
And I wanted to work on very
big- scale urban projects,

00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:51.250
because in Korea at the time,
cities for 300,000 people were

00:13:51.250 --> 00:13:52.510
being built over night.

00:13:52.510 --> 00:13:56.060
And I thought, well, we need
designers to think about that.

00:13:56.060 --> 00:13:59.340
After a year of working at an
urban research institute, I

00:13:59.340 --> 00:14:02.230
thought that is an important
cause, but

00:14:02.230 --> 00:14:03.460
that's not what I love.

00:14:03.460 --> 00:14:08.580
I love cities, but I want to
do it hands-on, not through

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:14.170
policy and not through sketches
that will take time

00:14:14.170 --> 00:14:18.090
to turn into a master plan
for traffic engineers

00:14:18.090 --> 00:14:19.980
to resolve, et cetera.

00:14:19.980 --> 00:14:26.270
I wanted a more immediate way
of changing the urban

00:14:26.270 --> 00:14:28.450
environment.

00:14:28.450 --> 00:14:31.380
And I thought you could change
urban environment through

00:14:31.380 --> 00:14:33.600
small-scale interventions.

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:41.960
So I started my own kind of
practice doing installation

00:14:41.960 --> 00:14:43.210
work in public space.

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:46.590
 

00:14:46.590 --> 00:14:50.760
One of the maybe most known
projects is a project called

00:14:50.760 --> 00:14:54.970
White Noise/White Light for
the Athens 2004 Olympics.

00:14:54.970 --> 00:14:57.020
But that doesn't explain
how I got to MIT.

00:14:57.020 --> 00:15:00.860
So I came to MIT in 2001.

00:15:00.860 --> 00:15:05.610
And I came out of an invitation
to apply for a

00:15:05.610 --> 00:15:09.000
junior faculty position
in architecture.

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:13.510
And what's amazing is I think
MIT changed my life.

00:15:13.510 --> 00:15:17.570
Not only that it gave me a job,
but it changed the course

00:15:17.570 --> 00:15:22.980
of the way I thought about
architecture and the city.

00:15:22.980 --> 00:15:23.960
INTERVIEWER: You've been
here 10 years now.

00:15:23.960 --> 00:15:24.460
So how did MIT--

00:15:24.460 --> 00:15:27.450
YOON: I've been here 10-- so
when I came in 2001, as I

00:15:27.450 --> 00:15:30.280
mentioned, I had a very
traditional architectural and

00:15:30.280 --> 00:15:34.150
urban design education, one
that was devoid of any

00:15:34.150 --> 00:15:39.540
technology in terms of the
way I was trained.

00:15:39.540 --> 00:15:46.820
I was not exposed to so many
things about design and

00:15:46.820 --> 00:15:49.490
technology in terms of
computer-aided design or

00:15:49.490 --> 00:15:51.310
manufacturing, et cetera.

00:15:51.310 --> 00:15:55.450
So when I came here in 2001,
because MIT has that word

00:15:55.450 --> 00:15:59.110
technology in it, I
was very nervous.

00:15:59.110 --> 00:16:02.900
And so my first semester of
teaching, I also signed up to

00:16:02.900 --> 00:16:03.800
be a student.

00:16:03.800 --> 00:16:07.250
So I took a course at the Media
Lab called, How To Make

00:16:07.250 --> 00:16:11.720
Almost Anything, which is taught
by Neil Gershenfeld

00:16:11.720 --> 00:16:17.580
because I thought, I'm so
technophobic, I should take a

00:16:17.580 --> 00:16:22.890
course that makes me less
fearful of technology.

00:16:22.890 --> 00:16:25.365
So during that first semester
I was teaching a studio, and

00:16:25.365 --> 00:16:27.220
then simultaneously
I was a student.

00:16:27.220 --> 00:16:30.960
And MIT has this great benefit
where they pay your tuition if

00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:34.890
you take a course, but you have
to get a B or above to

00:16:34.890 --> 00:16:36.930
get reimbursed for
that course.

00:16:36.930 --> 00:16:40.320
So it was like the only course I
actually ever cared about my

00:16:40.320 --> 00:16:43.630
grade, because I was worried
that I wouldn't get it

00:16:43.630 --> 00:16:44.160
reimbursed.

00:16:44.160 --> 00:16:48.040
But I got an A. And I learned
a lot through that class,

00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:52.650
because it was a crash course
in computer-aided design,

00:16:52.650 --> 00:16:57.320
manufacturing, basic
electronics, microcontrollers,

00:16:57.320 --> 00:16:58.930
prototyping.

00:16:58.930 --> 00:17:03.870
And the final project of that
class, for my final assignment

00:17:03.870 --> 00:17:06.790
I created something called
the Defensible Dress.

00:17:06.790 --> 00:17:11.254
And later iterations of that
dress were exhibited at the LA

00:17:11.254 --> 00:17:14.770
MOCA and the National
Museum in Tokyo.

00:17:14.770 --> 00:17:17.319
But the idea behind the dress
was I want to defend my

00:17:17.319 --> 00:17:19.079
personal space from people that

00:17:19.079 --> 00:17:21.530
crowd it in urban contexts.

00:17:21.530 --> 00:17:24.339
And so when anyone entered
a space zone

00:17:24.339 --> 00:17:26.440
that I could preset--

00:17:26.440 --> 00:17:29.220
24 inches was my comfort zone.

00:17:29.220 --> 00:17:32.100
I think culturally it's
different if you're in Spain

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:33.710
versus Tokyo.

00:17:33.710 --> 00:17:37.590
So the idea was you could
program your distance, your

00:17:37.590 --> 00:17:38.920
personal space distance.

00:17:38.920 --> 00:17:44.430
If anyone violated that, this
dress lifted like a porcupine

00:17:44.430 --> 00:17:47.455
with these spikes, and
kept people away.

00:17:47.455 --> 00:17:49.470
INTERVIEWER: Did you ever
wear it in public?

00:17:49.470 --> 00:17:50.410
YOON: I did wear it in public.

00:17:50.410 --> 00:17:52.210
And I had students wear
it in public.

00:17:52.210 --> 00:17:55.400
And it was shown on a mannequin
in the museums.

00:17:55.400 --> 00:17:57.070
INTERVIEWER: What was that
experience like, just briefly,

00:17:57.070 --> 00:17:58.010
and for the students,
especially.

00:17:58.010 --> 00:18:01.700
YOON: I think people were
like, what the?

00:18:01.700 --> 00:18:03.430
They were kind of
surprised by it.

00:18:03.430 --> 00:18:06.850
But at the same time I think it
resonated with everybody at

00:18:06.850 --> 00:18:07.310
the same time.

00:18:07.310 --> 00:18:11.520
It's a crazy project, but
everyone has had in public

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:15.160
space a feeling of kind of
personal space encroachment.

00:18:15.160 --> 00:18:20.920
And that was the project that
you can deal with kind of big-

00:18:20.920 --> 00:18:26.100
scale issues through a small,
personal scale intervention.

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:28.000
And that's what led to the
White Noise/White Light

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:30.090
project for the Athens
Olympics.

00:18:30.090 --> 00:18:35.230
That's what has led to a lot
of my work that deals with

00:18:35.230 --> 00:18:42.070
interactive design and
alternative energy and

00:18:42.070 --> 00:18:43.930
transforming a public space.

00:18:43.930 --> 00:18:45.560
INTERVIEWER: So I'm going to
digress one more step.

00:18:45.560 --> 00:18:47.780
And then we'll come back
to how MIT changed you.

00:18:47.780 --> 00:18:50.970
So in teaching, how do students
respond to this?

00:18:50.970 --> 00:18:55.190
I'm thinking of-- and I may have
a wrong stereotype of the

00:18:55.190 --> 00:19:01.580
engineer, where everything has
to be quantified and logical

00:19:01.580 --> 00:19:02.440
and explained.

00:19:02.440 --> 00:19:05.280
This idea of the Defensible
Dress, a big abstract idea and

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:08.140
a concrete object and the effect
of it is hard to put

00:19:08.140 --> 00:19:08.570
into words.

00:19:08.570 --> 00:19:10.050
Art is often beyond words.

00:19:10.050 --> 00:19:12.590
How do you translate that to
students and how do they

00:19:12.590 --> 00:19:13.235
respond to it?

00:19:13.235 --> 00:19:15.910
Do you get resistance?

00:19:15.910 --> 00:19:18.200
YOON: I feel like at MIT you
don't get resistance.

00:19:18.200 --> 00:19:20.950
I mean, I think the first
resistance would be a person

00:19:20.950 --> 00:19:23.800
that would ask, well, why would
you want to do that?

00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:25.580
But I think MIT--

00:19:25.580 --> 00:19:28.730
I've never found anyone that
asks me, why would

00:19:28.730 --> 00:19:30.166
you want to do that?

00:19:30.166 --> 00:19:32.730
It's, huh, that's really
strange, but that's kind of

00:19:32.730 --> 00:19:33.430
interesting.

00:19:33.430 --> 00:19:35.990
So how are you going
to do that?

00:19:35.990 --> 00:19:39.730
And I think that's the nature of
Neil's course, how to make

00:19:39.730 --> 00:19:44.010
almost anything, not what
should we make?

00:19:44.010 --> 00:19:45.740
Why should we make it?

00:19:45.740 --> 00:19:53.090
So I think in a way Neil's
course coupled with the kind

00:19:53.090 --> 00:20:00.760
of just general fearlessness
of MIT--

00:20:00.760 --> 00:20:10.770
like there is the attitude of
not why, but why not, and a

00:20:10.770 --> 00:20:13.090
lot of support.

00:20:13.090 --> 00:20:16.200
So I could take a class
as a faculty member.

00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:20.950
I could learn about things that
I was sort of afraid to

00:20:20.950 --> 00:20:24.320
admit that I didn't know much
about when I started here.

00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:28.362
And it did change the trajectory
of my work.

00:20:28.362 --> 00:20:31.250
And it could only have
happened here.

00:20:31.250 --> 00:20:35.790
If I had taken a teaching
position at any other school,

00:20:35.790 --> 00:20:39.884
my work would not be the
work it is today.

00:20:39.884 --> 00:20:40.940
INTERVIEWER: You've lost
your technophobia.

00:20:40.940 --> 00:20:42.520
Are you a technophile now?

00:20:42.520 --> 00:20:46.750
YOON: Well, I would say I am
still wary of technology.

00:20:46.750 --> 00:20:49.700
But I think that wariness is
a super-healthy aspect

00:20:49.700 --> 00:20:51.800
of the work, too.

00:20:51.800 --> 00:20:57.420
But I am fearless because
I now know

00:20:57.420 --> 00:20:58.590
people who are fearless.

00:20:58.590 --> 00:21:00.640
You know, once we were
doing a project.

00:21:00.640 --> 00:21:04.810
It was a sound installation,
used capacity sensing.

00:21:04.810 --> 00:21:08.150
And it produced sound, so I was
like, well, we should get

00:21:08.150 --> 00:21:11.680
a small MP3 player.

00:21:11.680 --> 00:21:13.970
And the engineer I was working
with was like, why would we

00:21:13.970 --> 00:21:15.380
buy an MP3 player?

00:21:15.380 --> 00:21:17.640
I'm going to just make
it from scratch.

00:21:17.640 --> 00:21:22.010
And that kind of attitude,
that MIT has people who

00:21:22.010 --> 00:21:26.450
actually still make things,
is pretty rare.

00:21:26.450 --> 00:21:28.230
We don't just talk
about things.

00:21:28.230 --> 00:21:30.820
We don't just philosophize
about things.

00:21:30.820 --> 00:21:32.150
We make things.

00:21:32.150 --> 00:21:32.800
We make things.

00:21:32.800 --> 00:21:33.750
We test things.

00:21:33.750 --> 00:21:37.400
And everything we make hopefully
impacts the world

00:21:37.400 --> 00:21:43.670
from the artist side to the
engineers that invent devices

00:21:43.670 --> 00:21:45.620
that help people.

00:21:45.620 --> 00:21:47.430
INTERVIEWER: MIT, learn
to be fearless.

00:21:47.430 --> 00:21:48.490
That's a pretty good slogan.

00:21:48.490 --> 00:21:49.300
YOON: Yes.

00:21:49.300 --> 00:21:50.060
Yes.

00:21:50.060 --> 00:21:52.370
INTERVIEWER: So now I want to
flip it around and ask the

00:21:52.370 --> 00:21:53.750
other side of the question
that I think is actually

00:21:53.750 --> 00:21:57.150
harder to answer, which is,
you've been here a decade.

00:21:57.150 --> 00:22:01.740
How have you influenced MIT?

00:22:01.740 --> 00:22:04.690
YOON: I don't know if
one person like me

00:22:04.690 --> 00:22:06.410
can influence MIT.

00:22:06.410 --> 00:22:11.470
I mean, I think that over the
decade I have seen change in

00:22:11.470 --> 00:22:12.430
my department.

00:22:12.430 --> 00:22:15.900
In the Department of
Architecture there is more

00:22:15.900 --> 00:22:22.660
openness to interdisciplinary
collaborations, more openness

00:22:22.660 --> 00:22:28.550
to projects that typically
would be seen on the very

00:22:28.550 --> 00:22:33.310
periphery of architectural
design, because it's dealing

00:22:33.310 --> 00:22:38.530
with either urban issues
or landscape issues or

00:22:38.530 --> 00:22:43.420
interactive design issues
or mobility issues.

00:22:43.420 --> 00:22:46.570
And I don't know if I'm
responsible for that or just

00:22:46.570 --> 00:22:52.380
over the last decade all the
departments have become more

00:22:52.380 --> 00:22:56.110
interdisciplinary, because the
problems now that exist in the

00:22:56.110 --> 00:23:00.610
world are such that no one
discipline can solve them.

00:23:00.610 --> 00:23:06.520
It requires the collaboration
of a planner and an

00:23:06.520 --> 00:23:14.130
environmental scientist and a
climatologist, or a geologist

00:23:14.130 --> 00:23:20.220
and an electrical engineer
and an artist, maybe.

00:23:20.220 --> 00:23:25.360
INTERVIEWER: So as head of the
undergraduate program here,

00:23:25.360 --> 00:23:30.200
does that influence where you
feel architecture education

00:23:30.200 --> 00:23:33.070
should go and how you're
planning the program for the

00:23:33.070 --> 00:23:35.030
next 5, 10, 15, 20 years?

00:23:35.030 --> 00:23:35.076
YOON: Yes.

00:23:35.076 --> 00:23:40.080
I mean, I think that the
undergrads are the best asset

00:23:40.080 --> 00:23:41.160
here at MIT.

00:23:41.160 --> 00:23:46.890
They're incredibly bright and
energetic and unencumbered yet

00:23:46.890 --> 00:23:53.280
by the rules of society and the
rules of the profession.

00:23:53.280 --> 00:23:54.865
So they are fearless.

00:23:54.865 --> 00:23:58.100
 

00:23:58.100 --> 00:24:02.470
And for me, the important thing
about an undergraduate

00:24:02.470 --> 00:24:08.320
program in architecture should
be that we train students such

00:24:08.320 --> 00:24:12.300
that if they want to go on and
get a master of architecture,

00:24:12.300 --> 00:24:15.180
they have a great foundation
to do so.

00:24:15.180 --> 00:24:20.530
But we don't create a program
that railroads them into only

00:24:20.530 --> 00:24:26.110
getting an architecture graduate
degree, that the

00:24:26.110 --> 00:24:32.680
education really focuses on
design thinking in a broader

00:24:32.680 --> 00:24:37.680
sense, so that they can go
on to doing anything from

00:24:37.680 --> 00:24:42.550
management problem solving
to industrial design to

00:24:42.550 --> 00:24:43.830
architecture.

00:24:43.830 --> 00:24:48.805
And historically, we have had
something like 30 percent, 40

00:24:48.805 --> 00:24:52.680
percent go on into
other fields.

00:24:52.680 --> 00:24:54.840
So it's, I think, different
from some undergraduate

00:24:54.840 --> 00:24:58.720
programs where like 90 percent
of the students go into that

00:24:58.720 --> 00:25:01.370
area for their Master's
degree.

00:25:01.370 --> 00:25:06.480
So it makes it a challenging
program to develop, because

00:25:06.480 --> 00:25:13.880
you want to make sure the
students have enough exposure

00:25:13.880 --> 00:25:17.720
to the kinds of course
curriculum that would prepare

00:25:17.720 --> 00:25:20.390
them for a master
of architecture.

00:25:20.390 --> 00:25:25.400
But you want to also make sure
that they're understanding

00:25:25.400 --> 00:25:28.990
architecture in the broadest
sense, that it is constructing

00:25:28.990 --> 00:25:32.030
the environment as
opposed to like

00:25:32.030 --> 00:25:35.390
building a kind of building.

00:25:35.390 --> 00:25:40.420
And I think we try hard to do
that by requiring courses in

00:25:40.420 --> 00:25:44.710
both art culture and technology
and building

00:25:44.710 --> 00:25:45.620
technology.

00:25:45.620 --> 00:25:51.070
Our students have to take both
so that they understand that

00:25:51.070 --> 00:25:56.470
architecture really does
sit between art and

00:25:56.470 --> 00:25:57.180
science/technology.

00:25:57.180 --> 00:25:59.850
It's like right smack
in the middle there.

00:25:59.850 --> 00:26:02.880
And I think the best
architects have a

00:26:02.880 --> 00:26:05.460
strong grasp of both.

00:26:05.460 --> 00:26:08.800
INTERVIEWER: And even, as you
say, it's part of a liberal

00:26:08.800 --> 00:26:09.550
arts education.

00:26:09.550 --> 00:26:10.840
You can do anything from it.

00:26:10.840 --> 00:26:13.980
It strikes me as you're talking
that as we go along

00:26:13.980 --> 00:26:15.400
into the future, society
gets more

00:26:15.400 --> 00:26:18.000
technological, more visual.

00:26:18.000 --> 00:26:22.200
And the difference between
design- thinking and thinking,

00:26:22.200 --> 00:26:24.540
it seems to me, is getting
less and less.

00:26:24.540 --> 00:26:28.130
And design is very useful.

00:26:28.130 --> 00:26:30.740
If you have design principles,
it applies to

00:26:30.740 --> 00:26:32.065
great number of fields.

00:26:32.065 --> 00:26:32.990
Is that true, do you think ?

00:26:32.990 --> 00:26:33.240
YOON: Yes.

00:26:33.240 --> 00:26:37.630
I mean, I think for me design-
thinking, it starts with how

00:26:37.630 --> 00:26:42.390
to analyze a problem and a
context and break that down.

00:26:42.390 --> 00:26:47.830
And it requires thinking
strategically and iteratively

00:26:47.830 --> 00:26:53.180
about multiple ways of attacking
that problem through

00:26:53.180 --> 00:26:55.790
the production of something.

00:26:55.790 --> 00:27:00.610
So I think that's where we are
designers and architects,

00:27:00.610 --> 00:27:05.660
because our goal is actually
to make something that is

00:27:05.660 --> 00:27:11.190
inhabitable or spatially
experienceable that changes

00:27:11.190 --> 00:27:13.000
the context.

00:27:13.000 --> 00:27:15.550
INTERVIEWER: Tell me a story,
one of your favorite stories

00:27:15.550 --> 00:27:18.820
of your undergrads, project
or success stories.

00:27:18.820 --> 00:27:23.680
Anything that stands
out to you.

00:27:23.680 --> 00:27:28.160
YOON: We've had some amazing,
amazing undergrads.

00:27:28.160 --> 00:27:34.130
There is a graduate of our
program named Lisa Smith who

00:27:34.130 --> 00:27:35.740
was one of my favorite
students.

00:27:35.740 --> 00:27:40.910
And I met her initially because
she was put on--

00:27:40.910 --> 00:27:43.050
I don't remember what
MIT calls it.

00:27:43.050 --> 00:27:48.960
But she had some disciplinary
hearing or something.

00:27:48.960 --> 00:27:52.590
So my first day of studio, she's
like, Professor Yoon,

00:27:52.590 --> 00:27:55.830
I'm sorry I have to leave 30
minutes early because I have a

00:27:55.830 --> 00:28:00.370
disciplinary hearing about
something I did.

00:28:00.370 --> 00:28:01.720
And I was like, oh
my goodness.

00:28:01.720 --> 00:28:04.340
She's going to be
a troublemaker.

00:28:04.340 --> 00:28:11.000
It turns out she had created a
survey using MIT letterhead.

00:28:11.000 --> 00:28:14.480
But the survey was kind
of a joke survey.

00:28:14.480 --> 00:28:18.560
So it was kind of very clever
and funny, but because it had

00:28:18.560 --> 00:28:23.310
the MIT logo on it,
she got into some

00:28:23.310 --> 00:28:25.610
disciplinary problems.

00:28:25.610 --> 00:28:30.170
But she created this megaphone
project where she got into

00:28:30.170 --> 00:28:35.070
trouble again, because as a
public art piece on the

00:28:35.070 --> 00:28:40.490
streets, she was screaming from
a megaphone about certain

00:28:40.490 --> 00:28:43.560
political issues.

00:28:43.560 --> 00:28:48.970
And to me she represents the
perfect spirit of MIT

00:28:48.970 --> 00:28:52.310
undergrads who want to change
the world and are using--

00:28:52.310 --> 00:28:54.080
and yes, they get into
some trouble.

00:28:54.080 --> 00:28:57.700
But I think the reason they're
getting into that trouble is

00:28:57.700 --> 00:29:02.880
because they're really trying to
make other people aware of

00:29:02.880 --> 00:29:08.195
certain conditions and finding
ways through that awareness to

00:29:08.195 --> 00:29:10.930
then make positive change.

00:29:10.930 --> 00:29:13.030
INTERVIEWER: So was the
megaphone project her final

00:29:13.030 --> 00:29:16.050
project or did you work with her
on developing things maybe

00:29:16.050 --> 00:29:18.330
so she could do these things
without getting arrested or

00:29:18.330 --> 00:29:20.130
did you encourage her
to get arrested?

00:29:20.130 --> 00:29:23.470
YOON: Well, you know, I didn't
encourage her to get arrested.

00:29:23.470 --> 00:29:30.640
But I supported her in finding
ways to get her projects done.

00:29:30.640 --> 00:29:34.080
And she graduated from here and
then she went to the Art

00:29:34.080 --> 00:29:36.890
Institute of Chicago,
where she got a

00:29:36.890 --> 00:29:40.160
master's in design objects.

00:29:40.160 --> 00:29:45.120
And now she's a very successful
designer, but she

00:29:45.120 --> 00:29:48.240
designs polemical objects.

00:29:48.240 --> 00:29:50.180
INTERVIEWER: What's a
polemical object?

00:29:50.180 --> 00:29:53.120
 

00:29:53.120 --> 00:29:59.280
YOON: She designed these mats,
but they have a message to

00:29:59.280 --> 00:30:03.160
them, a political message to
them that isn't just text, but

00:30:03.160 --> 00:30:06.200
the perverseness of the thing.

00:30:06.200 --> 00:30:11.580
Or a lamp that's designed so
minimally that it's a line.

00:30:11.580 --> 00:30:13.580
So it doesn't look
like a lamp.

00:30:13.580 --> 00:30:16.490
It actually just changes your
reading of space and

00:30:16.490 --> 00:30:18.830
perspective.

00:30:18.830 --> 00:30:20.900
INTERVIEWER: So we've touched
on this a little, but in

00:30:20.900 --> 00:30:22.600
looking forward just
architecture schools in

00:30:22.600 --> 00:30:25.880
general over the next decades,
what's the biggest challenge?

00:30:25.880 --> 00:30:28.620
YOON: I think the biggest
challenge is going to be in

00:30:28.620 --> 00:30:30.490
dealing with environmental
issues.

00:30:30.490 --> 00:30:33.380
 

00:30:33.380 --> 00:30:37.380
Buildings, of course, take
up the most energy.

00:30:37.380 --> 00:30:41.330
If you look at charts about what
consumes the most energy

00:30:41.330 --> 00:30:46.410
in the United States, it's not
cars and it's not trains and

00:30:46.410 --> 00:30:47.330
it's not planes.

00:30:47.330 --> 00:30:49.200
It's actually buildings.

00:30:49.200 --> 00:30:53.500
So I think that's going to be
the biggest concern for the

00:30:53.500 --> 00:30:59.620
next generation, how to design
in such a way that our

00:30:59.620 --> 00:31:04.520
buildings are smart and
intelligent, reduce energy

00:31:04.520 --> 00:31:10.400
use, while still creating
amazing environments for us to

00:31:10.400 --> 00:31:13.160
do things in, creating
collaborative environments,

00:31:13.160 --> 00:31:15.695
because more of those kinds of
spaces will be necessary.

00:31:15.695 --> 00:31:18.790
 

00:31:18.790 --> 00:31:21.390
INTERVIEWER: But on the other
hand, I've read that urban

00:31:21.390 --> 00:31:23.800
centers in places like New
York in some ways are the

00:31:23.800 --> 00:31:25.710
greenest places of all because
there's so much density.

00:31:25.710 --> 00:31:27.120
YOON: Yes, because
of the density.

00:31:27.120 --> 00:31:27.850
Yes.

00:31:27.850 --> 00:31:30.600
But in the United States, I
don't think we'll ever have a

00:31:30.600 --> 00:31:36.300
condition where there'll
be 12 Manhattans.

00:31:36.300 --> 00:31:41.810
So I do think architects need
to think about how to build

00:31:41.810 --> 00:31:45.320
sustainably at all
kinds of scales.

00:31:45.320 --> 00:31:51.510
Like at the suburban scale,
because the car is part of the

00:31:51.510 --> 00:31:54.610
American dream and until we
change the American dream,

00:31:54.610 --> 00:31:56.340
we're going to have suburbs.

00:31:56.340 --> 00:32:00.620
So in a suburban context, what
can we do to create the

00:32:00.620 --> 00:32:02.895
maximum amount of energy
efficiency?

00:32:02.895 --> 00:32:07.410
 

00:32:07.410 --> 00:32:09.350
INTERVIEWER: In the graduate
level architecture design

00:32:09.350 --> 00:32:13.250
studio course you've been
teaching for the last decade,

00:32:13.250 --> 00:32:15.800
you ask your students to quote,
"think design through

00:32:15.800 --> 00:32:17.890
systems."

00:32:17.890 --> 00:32:20.940
YOON: Where did you here that?

00:32:20.940 --> 00:32:21.490
INTERVIEWER: It's been
given to me.

00:32:21.490 --> 00:32:23.680
Referring to the challenge
issued by architecture critic,

00:32:23.680 --> 00:32:26.750
Reyner Banham.

00:32:26.750 --> 00:32:29.090
So can you tell us a little more
about that and how that

00:32:29.090 --> 00:32:30.648
works in the context of MIT?

00:32:30.648 --> 00:32:33.580
YOON: Yes.

00:32:33.580 --> 00:32:36.810
I think it's very important to
think through systems, through

00:32:36.810 --> 00:32:41.630
designing systems, as opposed
to designing a one-off

00:32:41.630 --> 00:32:45.540
exquisite design object.

00:32:45.540 --> 00:32:47.400
INTERVIEWER: I've heard the
masterpiece is dead.

00:32:47.400 --> 00:32:48.900
YOON: Well, it's not--

00:32:48.900 --> 00:32:50.890
I mean, I love masterpieces
still.

00:32:50.890 --> 00:32:56.590
But the masterpiece only
addresses one percent of

00:32:56.590 --> 00:32:58.010
architecture, if that.

00:32:58.010 --> 00:33:03.860
Maybe you 0.1 percent of one
percent of architecture are

00:33:03.860 --> 00:33:07.680
the masterpieces, especially
in the United States.

00:33:07.680 --> 00:33:15.240
But if you can design a system,
whether that system is

00:33:15.240 --> 00:33:20.530
a way of design, or if you can
design through organizing

00:33:20.530 --> 00:33:26.180
structural, mechanical, and
programmatic systems in a way

00:33:26.180 --> 00:33:31.780
that work smartly and still
produce amazing spaces, I

00:33:31.780 --> 00:33:37.250
think that's the key, as
opposed to all the top

00:33:37.250 --> 00:33:40.130
architects producing one-offs.

00:33:40.130 --> 00:33:47.597
That is not going to help change
the environment that 99

00:33:47.597 --> 00:33:52.800
percent of the world, or 99
percent of the United States

00:33:52.800 --> 00:33:56.690
lives in, works in, plays in.

00:33:56.690 --> 00:33:57.810
INTERVIEWER: What's a
successful example?

00:33:57.810 --> 00:34:01.410
What's the most stand-out
example of a place or a system

00:34:01.410 --> 00:34:04.750
that's doing it right anywhere
in the world that we

00:34:04.750 --> 00:34:07.060
can look to as a--

00:34:07.060 --> 00:34:11.239
YOON: There are some great
historical examples, that I

00:34:11.239 --> 00:34:16.370
would say range from Hadrian's
Villa, where--

00:34:16.370 --> 00:34:17.330
INTERVIEWER: In ancient Rome.

00:34:17.330 --> 00:34:18.400
YOON: Yes.

00:34:18.400 --> 00:34:23.489
I mean, I think where space
is organized so

00:34:23.489 --> 00:34:25.400
that it deals with--

00:34:25.400 --> 00:34:28.080
in terms of the baths,
the hot, the cold.

00:34:28.080 --> 00:34:34.940
And there's an organization
to that and an integration

00:34:34.940 --> 00:34:39.440
between the architecture and the
technology of the time and

00:34:39.440 --> 00:34:42.960
the program in a really
integrated way.

00:34:42.960 --> 00:34:44.580
INTERVIEWER: By program we mean
that that's the place you

00:34:44.580 --> 00:34:45.690
go to have a bath.

00:34:45.690 --> 00:34:46.630
YOON: Yes.

00:34:46.630 --> 00:34:49.080
Yes.

00:34:49.080 --> 00:34:52.030
And to me, that is an integrated
system, so

00:34:52.030 --> 00:34:58.500
integrated that you can't take
one thing out without it all

00:34:58.500 --> 00:35:00.010
just kind of falling apart.

00:35:00.010 --> 00:35:02.950
 

00:35:02.950 --> 00:35:06.000
Maybe a more recent example
would be like the Centre

00:35:06.000 --> 00:35:07.900
Pompidou in Paris.

00:35:07.900 --> 00:35:10.290
But there, they codify
the system.

00:35:10.290 --> 00:35:14.140
So on the outside, you
see all the colors.

00:35:14.140 --> 00:35:16.340
And that's because they're
showing which ones are

00:35:16.340 --> 00:35:21.930
plumbing pipes and which ones
are mechanical ducts, and

00:35:21.930 --> 00:35:24.440
intake, outflow, et cetera.

00:35:24.440 --> 00:35:28.560
And that's a building that
literally turns inside out in

00:35:28.560 --> 00:35:30.930
order to make the public
kind of aware of the

00:35:30.930 --> 00:35:34.600
systems of a building.

00:35:34.600 --> 00:35:41.950
I think many architects, like
Foster's building in Hong

00:35:41.950 --> 00:35:46.580
Kong, the Hong Kong Shanghai
Bank, is a masterful example

00:35:46.580 --> 00:35:51.760
of how structural systems work
together in an integrated way.

00:35:51.760 --> 00:35:54.540
 

00:35:54.540 --> 00:35:55.090
INTERVIEWER: How about MIT?

00:35:55.090 --> 00:35:59.220
Does MIT work well as a
system of buildings?

00:35:59.220 --> 00:36:05.460
YOON: I think MIT is a kind
of campus of ad-hoc-ness.

00:36:05.460 --> 00:36:06.710
INTERVIEWER: It's a kludge.

00:36:06.710 --> 00:36:09.670
 

00:36:09.670 --> 00:36:13.520
YOON: It's not a system.

00:36:13.520 --> 00:36:17.380
 

00:36:17.380 --> 00:36:22.010
It's the aftermath of
piecemealing and making things

00:36:22.010 --> 00:36:24.340
work ad hoc, bit by bit.

00:36:24.340 --> 00:36:29.020
And I think as we move forward,
every time I'm

00:36:29.020 --> 00:36:33.640
outside on a street and I see
them digging up my street,

00:36:33.640 --> 00:36:37.120
because I live in downtown
Boston, they're digging it up,

00:36:37.120 --> 00:36:39.320
all the asphalt, to
run something.

00:36:39.320 --> 00:36:40.320
Who knows what it is?

00:36:40.320 --> 00:36:40.960
Bios.

00:36:40.960 --> 00:36:43.830
And then like three weeks later
they're digging it up

00:36:43.830 --> 00:36:47.790
and then somebody's running
maybe a gas line under there.

00:36:47.790 --> 00:36:50.970
And to me, that's what I mean
by it's not thought through

00:36:50.970 --> 00:36:52.570
like a system.

00:36:52.570 --> 00:36:57.590
It's always being dug up and
something added in, as opposed

00:36:57.590 --> 00:37:02.580
to thinking through what it
means to have infrastructure

00:37:02.580 --> 00:37:05.470
in an urban context embedded
with buildings.

00:37:05.470 --> 00:37:07.960
I think there is a
different way to

00:37:07.960 --> 00:37:09.195
think about that problem.

00:37:09.195 --> 00:37:11.100
INTERVIEWER: It seems like we're
coming to grips with

00:37:11.100 --> 00:37:13.980
change in architecture
as well.

00:37:13.980 --> 00:37:16.350
I was hearing a story the other
day about a brand new

00:37:16.350 --> 00:37:19.020
building that they spent years
and millions of dollars--

00:37:19.020 --> 00:37:19.310
or no.

00:37:19.310 --> 00:37:21.170
It was an old building that
they retrofitted so that

00:37:21.170 --> 00:37:22.670
everybody could have an
ethernet connection.

00:37:22.670 --> 00:37:25.450
By the time they finished,
they didn't need them.

00:37:25.450 --> 00:37:25.820
YOON: Yes.

00:37:25.820 --> 00:37:28.880
Technology leaps.

00:37:28.880 --> 00:37:31.950
And architecture is slow.

00:37:31.950 --> 00:37:34.170
So there are many buildings--

00:37:34.170 --> 00:37:38.880
like I think even the Stata
Center here at MIT, between

00:37:38.880 --> 00:37:42.220
when they started designing it
and when it was built was

00:37:42.220 --> 00:37:44.550
many, many, many years.

00:37:44.550 --> 00:37:46.650
And buildings take
a long time.

00:37:46.650 --> 00:37:50.300
They take a long time to design,
12 to 18 months.

00:37:50.300 --> 00:37:52.370
And then they take a
long time to build,

00:37:52.370 --> 00:37:55.660
another 18 to 24 months.

00:37:55.660 --> 00:37:58.360
By the time you add that all
up together from the minute

00:37:58.360 --> 00:38:02.250
you started to draw your first
line to when people are moving

00:38:02.250 --> 00:38:07.860
in, we've had four years of
technological revolution.

00:38:07.860 --> 00:38:11.010
So architecture will always
remain kind of-- because it's

00:38:11.010 --> 00:38:16.916
still built by humans,
still physical.

00:38:16.916 --> 00:38:21.430
So it will always be bridging
kind of low and high

00:38:21.430 --> 00:38:22.310
technologies.

00:38:22.310 --> 00:38:26.250
I think that's also what makes
architecture really amazing.

00:38:26.250 --> 00:38:29.550
I think of a wall as a
form of technology.

00:38:29.550 --> 00:38:33.100
It separates me from
whoever else.

00:38:33.100 --> 00:38:36.370
And the way you design the wall,
if you undulate the wall

00:38:36.370 --> 00:38:38.460
it has different acoustic
properties.

00:38:38.460 --> 00:38:43.300
If you splay the wall, it has
different optical qualities.

00:38:43.300 --> 00:38:47.450
So I still think the most basic
forms of architecture

00:38:47.450 --> 00:38:54.480
technology are incredibly
manipulatable.

00:38:54.480 --> 00:38:57.290
And so I think the challenge
of an architect now is we

00:38:57.290 --> 00:39:00.820
still have to design amazing
walls, but now we have to

00:39:00.820 --> 00:39:06.580
integrate them with
infrastructural systems and

00:39:06.580 --> 00:39:09.285
technologies that are
constantly changing.

00:39:09.285 --> 00:39:12.740
And it used to be people told
me that, oh, buildings are

00:39:12.740 --> 00:39:17.455
really only built for a lifespan
of like 30 years.

00:39:17.455 --> 00:39:20.370
But I think most people stay
in buildings much longer.

00:39:20.370 --> 00:39:25.140
The building I live in in Boston
was built in 1890.

00:39:25.140 --> 00:39:29.230
And so we have to think that our
buildings are not like 30

00:39:29.230 --> 00:39:30.910
years we're going to
just tear them down

00:39:30.910 --> 00:39:31.830
and build them again.

00:39:31.830 --> 00:39:38.700
But how can the building adapt
to transformation?

00:39:38.700 --> 00:39:41.820
INTERVIEWER: I'm always struck
by, this year especially at

00:39:41.820 --> 00:39:44.280
the Design School, how
buildings, as you say, they're

00:39:44.280 --> 00:39:48.940
both technology, but they're
ideas in space and time.

00:39:48.940 --> 00:39:51.570
And now this idea seems to
be flexibility, change,

00:39:51.570 --> 00:39:52.320
adaptability.

00:39:52.320 --> 00:39:53.570
YOON: Yes.

00:39:53.570 --> 00:39:55.470
 

00:39:55.470 --> 00:39:59.310
Every architecture project,
every good architecture

00:39:59.310 --> 00:40:01.050
project, is based on an idea.

00:40:01.050 --> 00:40:04.060
It's based on a concept.

00:40:04.060 --> 00:40:07.930
And I think we saw in the '60s
and '70s, especially in Japan

00:40:07.930 --> 00:40:12.460
after the war, a lot of research
into thinking about

00:40:12.460 --> 00:40:13.710
adaptability.

00:40:13.710 --> 00:40:15.730
 

00:40:15.730 --> 00:40:18.030
It was the Metabolist Movement
at the time.

00:40:18.030 --> 00:40:24.620
But adaptability,
transformation, a way to

00:40:24.620 --> 00:40:29.330
re-imagine the future of Tokyo,
big visions, and a lot

00:40:29.330 --> 00:40:35.890
of great heroic buildings that
researched these ideas.

00:40:35.890 --> 00:40:38.450
I think in the US we had
that moment too in the

00:40:38.450 --> 00:40:40.100
'60s and the '70s.

00:40:40.100 --> 00:40:43.010
And I think in Boston there
are many great--

00:40:43.010 --> 00:40:45.800
I would call them masterpieces,
but maybe some

00:40:45.800 --> 00:40:47.695
people in Boston would call
them abominations--

00:40:47.695 --> 00:40:50.850
 

00:40:50.850 --> 00:40:54.160
buildings that were really based
on an idea and testing

00:40:54.160 --> 00:40:55.060
those ideas.

00:40:55.060 --> 00:40:55.765
INTERVIEWER: You're not talking
about Government

00:40:55.765 --> 00:40:56.660
Center, are you?

00:40:56.660 --> 00:41:02.120
YOON: I'm talking about the
range from Exeter Library to

00:41:02.120 --> 00:41:06.880
the Government Center to
Rudolph's public service

00:41:06.880 --> 00:41:09.220
building on Cambridge Street.

00:41:09.220 --> 00:41:10.513
Yes.

00:41:10.513 --> 00:41:10.944
INTERVIEWER: Wow.

00:41:10.944 --> 00:41:13.330
Defend Government
Center for me.

00:41:13.330 --> 00:41:14.330
Change my mind.

00:41:14.330 --> 00:41:23.550
YOON: Well, I think the diagram
is interesting to--

00:41:23.550 --> 00:41:26.880
the first move to create such
a giant public plaza.

00:41:26.880 --> 00:41:30.740
I'm not saying it was done
right or that it works.

00:41:30.740 --> 00:41:34.550
But there was a clarity about
the idea that there would be a

00:41:34.550 --> 00:41:39.380
huge public plaza, huge,
based on Siena.

00:41:39.380 --> 00:41:42.890
They didn't quite get the
Siena part, but--

00:41:42.890 --> 00:41:47.020
and that the government building
would be kind of

00:41:47.020 --> 00:41:49.430
transparent to the public.

00:41:49.430 --> 00:41:53.700
The mayor's office would be
where it is, with the glass

00:41:53.700 --> 00:41:57.950
windows, that there'd be a giant
courtyard in the middle

00:41:57.950 --> 00:42:01.280
that brought daylight
internally, that the ground

00:42:01.280 --> 00:42:06.810
level would have all the public
administration for

00:42:06.810 --> 00:42:11.030
taxes and whatever, for people
to go pay their bills or

00:42:11.030 --> 00:42:13.930
whatever they do down
on the lower level.

00:42:13.930 --> 00:42:16.970
So it was meant to be an
incredibly porous building.

00:42:16.970 --> 00:42:19.540
It didn't work that way, because
I think after it was

00:42:19.540 --> 00:42:22.870
built they closed off most of
the entries and so it turned a

00:42:22.870 --> 00:42:26.220
circulation that was supposed
to be about porosity for the

00:42:26.220 --> 00:42:29.580
public into kind of a labyrinth

00:42:29.580 --> 00:42:31.860
because of security issues.

00:42:31.860 --> 00:42:36.460
And the world changed between
the optimism of that

00:42:36.460 --> 00:42:41.230
public-ness to the realities
of security today.

00:42:41.230 --> 00:42:44.420
INTERVIEWER: I think it's been
most successful for me when

00:42:44.420 --> 00:42:47.320
they put up those big screens
when the Red Sox were in the

00:42:47.320 --> 00:42:50.370
World Series or something like
that and it does becomes this

00:42:50.370 --> 00:42:51.990
wonderfully warm, festive
civic place.

00:42:51.990 --> 00:42:52.650
YOON: Yes.

00:42:52.650 --> 00:42:57.040
I think in the United States,
you can't have public space

00:42:57.040 --> 00:43:01.290
without programming, without
something happening in that

00:43:01.290 --> 00:43:03.480
space, because--

00:43:03.480 --> 00:43:06.680
and especially in Boston-- we
don't necessarily have the

00:43:06.680 --> 00:43:11.720
culture or the climate to
support the public just

00:43:11.720 --> 00:43:17.180
hanging out in a plaza like
Boston City Hall's.

00:43:17.180 --> 00:43:20.660
INTERVIEWER: So what keeps
coming up in your work really

00:43:20.660 --> 00:43:21.980
is civic engagement.

00:43:21.980 --> 00:43:27.240
It's really using art and
architecture to promote civic

00:43:27.240 --> 00:43:28.910
engagement.

00:43:28.910 --> 00:43:31.400
How has that mission evolved
in your field,

00:43:31.400 --> 00:43:32.830
and where is it going?

00:43:32.830 --> 00:43:34.050
Are we going to see
more and more--

00:43:34.050 --> 00:43:41.460
YOON: Well, I mean, I think
there are all kinds of

00:43:41.460 --> 00:43:42.510
architectures.

00:43:42.510 --> 00:43:46.500
But I think architecture
is most

00:43:46.500 --> 00:43:50.030
present in a public context.

00:43:50.030 --> 00:43:59.750
So it used to be that the
Forum in Rome, like that

00:43:59.750 --> 00:44:04.920
architecture made civic space.

00:44:04.920 --> 00:44:06.750
It helped define civic space.

00:44:06.750 --> 00:44:10.590
It helped foster civic space.

00:44:10.590 --> 00:44:13.940
For me, I'm very interested now,
because we have less a

00:44:13.940 --> 00:44:17.720
notion of civic space in the
United States than maybe a

00:44:17.720 --> 00:44:21.790
more neutral word like
public space.

00:44:21.790 --> 00:44:26.310
But I'm very interested in how
architecture and public space

00:44:26.310 --> 00:44:35.330
work together, how public
architectures can help change

00:44:35.330 --> 00:44:39.120
the mission of an institute.

00:44:39.120 --> 00:44:42.290
We just finished the new
headquarters for the Boston

00:44:42.290 --> 00:44:46.050
Society of Architects
in our practice.

00:44:46.050 --> 00:44:51.770
And the BSA moved from Broad
Street to Atlantic Wharf.

00:44:51.770 --> 00:44:57.350
And with that move, they are
leasing a space which is about

00:44:57.350 --> 00:45:00.970
15,000 square feet which
has almost all of

00:45:00.970 --> 00:45:02.790
that on a second four.

00:45:02.790 --> 00:45:06.390
And the ground floor is about
1,000 square feet.

00:45:06.390 --> 00:45:11.000
But it's got window front, but
it's on the second floor.

00:45:11.000 --> 00:45:15.170
So that project for us was all
about how can we make a second

00:45:15.170 --> 00:45:18.520
floor space, because it's
a gallery, seem

00:45:18.520 --> 00:45:20.810
as public as possible?

00:45:20.810 --> 00:45:24.250
And it's a Chapter 91 space, so
it needs to be public and

00:45:24.250 --> 00:45:26.085
it needs to be accessible--

00:45:26.085 --> 00:45:27.157
INTERVIEWER: Chapter 91?

00:45:27.157 --> 00:45:28.380
YOON: I don't actually know.

00:45:28.380 --> 00:45:30.240
INTERVIEWER: Just if you get
money from the State you have

00:45:30.240 --> 00:45:32.240
to have it accessible
to the public.

00:45:32.240 --> 00:45:32.580
YOON: Yes.

00:45:32.580 --> 00:45:35.070
And it has to be programmed
as public space.

00:45:35.070 --> 00:45:39.170
So it is an architecture
gallery.

00:45:39.170 --> 00:45:43.570
And our idea was that we would
create a move that would try

00:45:43.570 --> 00:45:47.785
to make that second floor space
seem part of street,

00:45:47.785 --> 00:45:49.260
which was very challenging.

00:45:49.260 --> 00:45:53.970
So what we did was we gave up
all the perimeter of that

00:45:53.970 --> 00:45:57.070
space to the public programming,
to the galleries.

00:45:57.070 --> 00:46:01.360
We pushed all the staff offices
and conference rooms

00:46:01.360 --> 00:46:06.710
back off the glass so that
you could, from the

00:46:06.710 --> 00:46:08.810
ground floor, look up.

00:46:08.810 --> 00:46:12.760
And we created this green soffit
in the ceiling, so that

00:46:12.760 --> 00:46:15.680
ceiling acted kind of as a
facade, because you could see

00:46:15.680 --> 00:46:17.040
it from the street.

00:46:17.040 --> 00:46:20.960
And then that ceiling like
scooped down to make a grand

00:46:20.960 --> 00:46:23.110
stair that people
kind of walk up.

00:46:23.110 --> 00:46:26.040
And it's painted bright green
so you can't miss it.

00:46:26.040 --> 00:46:28.180
And the idea is you scoop
up the public.

00:46:28.180 --> 00:46:32.010
They end up on the second floor
space and they see the

00:46:32.010 --> 00:46:35.200
architecture exhibits in the
context of the city.

00:46:35.200 --> 00:46:39.410
So they can look right out the
window and see what design is

00:46:39.410 --> 00:46:42.810
doing is trying to make an
impact and change in the

00:46:42.810 --> 00:46:46.590
physical world we inhabit.

00:46:46.590 --> 00:46:48.220
INTERVIEWER: You're both a
working architect and a

00:46:48.220 --> 00:46:50.120
working artist and teacher.

00:46:50.120 --> 00:46:52.045
Do your students get
involved in your

00:46:52.045 --> 00:46:53.200
architectural projects?

00:46:53.200 --> 00:46:53.550
YOON: Yes.

00:46:53.550 --> 00:46:55.560
We do take summer interns.

00:46:55.560 --> 00:46:58.930
And we hire many graduates
from the department.

00:46:58.930 --> 00:47:02.740
And then another way students
get involved is

00:47:02.740 --> 00:47:05.940
through UROP projects.

00:47:05.940 --> 00:47:11.770
So some of the design research
work I do, I hire

00:47:11.770 --> 00:47:12.960
undergraduates to help with.

00:47:12.960 --> 00:47:13.960
INTERVIEWER: UROP is?

00:47:13.960 --> 00:47:15.760
YOON: Undergraduate Research
Opportunity.

00:47:15.760 --> 00:47:19.980
It's a really special program
MIT has, where MIT, the

00:47:19.980 --> 00:47:26.390
Institute, supports faculty to
hire undergraduate research

00:47:26.390 --> 00:47:28.780
students for their projects.

00:47:28.780 --> 00:47:34.460
So I think typically faculty
would tend to hire graduate

00:47:34.460 --> 00:47:38.400
students over undergraduate
students, simply because they

00:47:38.400 --> 00:47:43.850
have more experience or they're
directly involved in

00:47:43.850 --> 00:47:45.710
their labs already.

00:47:45.710 --> 00:47:50.500
But MIT's program is really
trying to encourage undergrads

00:47:50.500 --> 00:47:54.340
and faculty to have a closer
relationship and give

00:47:54.340 --> 00:47:57.140
undergrads an experience they
wouldn't have otherwise.

00:47:57.140 --> 00:48:03.130
So MIT actually pays the
undergrads directly to work on

00:48:03.130 --> 00:48:04.750
faculty research projects.

00:48:04.750 --> 00:48:11.550
So one project that I used UROP
students on was a project

00:48:11.550 --> 00:48:17.010
called Unsolicited Small
Projects for the Big Dig.

00:48:17.010 --> 00:48:20.230
And it was a book project where
we did a lot of research

00:48:20.230 --> 00:48:26.170
on the Big Dig and the kind of
infrastructural overhaul of

00:48:26.170 --> 00:48:27.440
that project.

00:48:27.440 --> 00:48:31.540
And then we made speculative
conjectures on a series of

00:48:31.540 --> 00:48:37.670
projects that are very, very
hypothetical that could've

00:48:37.670 --> 00:48:44.620
happened or may one day happen
to make the Greenway more a

00:48:44.620 --> 00:48:46.400
vital public space.

00:48:46.400 --> 00:48:49.780
Because even though it is a
public space and it is a park

00:48:49.780 --> 00:48:56.800
and it is increasingly well
used, there was not much

00:48:56.800 --> 00:48:59.670
intention or--

00:48:59.670 --> 00:49:01.210
maybe intention is not
the right word.

00:49:01.210 --> 00:49:06.370
But there was not much
specificity in thinking about

00:49:06.370 --> 00:49:11.830
what could happen on those
parcels, those new parks, now

00:49:11.830 --> 00:49:15.320
that we had this huge
way of connecting

00:49:15.320 --> 00:49:17.550
the city back together.

00:49:17.550 --> 00:49:22.490
It's unprecedented as an urban
project in the United States.

00:49:22.490 --> 00:49:27.690
And very little, I think, was
thought about what you could

00:49:27.690 --> 00:49:31.870
do beyond just making it
a really nice park.

00:49:31.870 --> 00:49:32.540
INTERVIEWER: So what's
an example?

00:49:32.540 --> 00:49:33.270
What could you do?

00:49:33.270 --> 00:49:38.670
YOON: Well, like last year a
young architecture firm called

00:49:38.670 --> 00:49:44.030
Studio Luz produced a giant
hammock on one of the parcels,

00:49:44.030 --> 00:49:47.830
a hammock that like 20 people
could hang out on

00:49:47.830 --> 00:49:50.270
simultaneously.

00:49:50.270 --> 00:49:56.140
Some new interventions, like
the carousel, I think, has

00:49:56.140 --> 00:49:57.370
been successful.

00:49:57.370 --> 00:50:05.930
But I'm thinking more like why
isn't there like a parcel

00:50:05.930 --> 00:50:12.240
that's just 100 dirigibles?

00:50:12.240 --> 00:50:15.970
It is such a unique kind
of urban space.

00:50:15.970 --> 00:50:22.370
And I don't think the
activities on it are

00:50:22.370 --> 00:50:26.160
particularly wonder-filled
or unique.

00:50:26.160 --> 00:50:31.120
 

00:50:31.120 --> 00:50:34.350
It's public, but it doesn't
gather people let's say

00:50:34.350 --> 00:50:37.240
together, other than that water
fountain, which is super

00:50:37.240 --> 00:50:38.440
successful.

00:50:38.440 --> 00:50:40.423
INTERVIEWER: So we need a
Christo to come put the gates

00:50:40.423 --> 00:50:41.570
in and get us--

00:50:41.570 --> 00:50:42.310
YOON: Absolutely.

00:50:42.310 --> 00:50:45.250
I think once a year they should
have an artist do

00:50:45.250 --> 00:50:47.330
something that ties
all the parcels

00:50:47.330 --> 00:50:49.260
along the line together.

00:50:49.260 --> 00:50:52.830
And then other times there
could be different

00:50:52.830 --> 00:50:54.220
environmental transformations.

00:50:54.220 --> 00:50:56.150
I think the Harbor Island
Pavilion has

00:50:56.150 --> 00:50:57.820
been a great success.

00:50:57.820 --> 00:51:00.890
So I think over the years after
it opened there have

00:51:00.890 --> 00:51:06.050
been more experiments on how
to make the Greenway civic

00:51:06.050 --> 00:51:08.650
space as well as a park space.

00:51:08.650 --> 00:51:11.990
INTERVIEWER: Any reaction from
your unsolicited suggestions?

00:51:11.990 --> 00:51:15.640
YOON: Oh, I think people were
very nervous about it, because

00:51:15.640 --> 00:51:18.400
some of the proposals were
really crazy, because I am

00:51:18.400 --> 00:51:19.800
frustrated that there
are still so

00:51:19.800 --> 00:51:21.800
many lanes of traffic.

00:51:21.800 --> 00:51:25.310
And even though it's supposed
to be a linear park, because

00:51:25.310 --> 00:51:28.870
you have to cross a road every
time if you're trying to jog,

00:51:28.870 --> 00:51:31.430
it's not a really linear
experience.

00:51:31.430 --> 00:51:36.070
So I proposed in one of the
projects a jogger's E-ZPass.

00:51:36.070 --> 00:51:39.130
So you would put a little
E-ZPass on your ankle

00:51:39.130 --> 00:51:39.810
and you would jog.

00:51:39.810 --> 00:51:44.180
And as you jogged, it would
literally stop all the lights

00:51:44.180 --> 00:51:48.610
so that it privileged the
pedestrian, as opposed to our

00:51:48.610 --> 00:51:51.810
current environment which always
privileges the car.

00:51:51.810 --> 00:51:56.040
So as the pedestrian jogs, not
only do all the walk/don't

00:51:56.040 --> 00:51:59.900
walk signs work in their favor,
but a landscape of

00:51:59.900 --> 00:52:04.710
trees follows you, so you never
have to see the cars.

00:52:04.710 --> 00:52:06.710
So they run with you,
these trees.

00:52:06.710 --> 00:52:08.040
INTERVIEWER: I love that.

00:52:08.040 --> 00:52:09.540
Is it like a people-mover
with trees on it?

00:52:09.540 --> 00:52:10.850
How does it quite work?

00:52:10.850 --> 00:52:12.180
YOON: I didn't figure--

00:52:12.180 --> 00:52:13.740
it was just very speculative.

00:52:13.740 --> 00:52:24.150
But that book led to actually,
surprisingly, an invitation

00:52:24.150 --> 00:52:30.480
for me to join a working group
that is now thinking about

00:52:30.480 --> 00:52:34.490
public art on the Greenway,
public art and programming on

00:52:34.490 --> 00:52:35.070
the Greenway.

00:52:35.070 --> 00:52:38.970
So I think it led to
the right thing.

00:52:38.970 --> 00:52:40.090
INTERVIEWER: I've done a few of
these interviews now, and

00:52:40.090 --> 00:52:44.730
it seems there is a common theme
at MIT of striking out a

00:52:44.730 --> 00:52:46.760
huge vision, never mind whether
it can actually be

00:52:46.760 --> 00:52:50.350
done or not, but put
a flag out there.

00:52:50.350 --> 00:52:52.140
This is where we want to go.

00:52:52.140 --> 00:52:54.700
And then figure the reality
to get there.

00:52:54.700 --> 00:52:56.020
And you might not get
where you're going,

00:52:56.020 --> 00:52:57.360
but you'll get somewhere.

00:52:57.360 --> 00:52:57.810
YOON: Yes.

00:52:57.810 --> 00:52:58.650
I think that's right.

00:52:58.650 --> 00:53:06.340
I think that somehow MIT
encourages the big, big ideas,

00:53:06.340 --> 00:53:07.470
the big visions.

00:53:07.470 --> 00:53:12.750
But we're not clowns.

00:53:12.750 --> 00:53:15.680
We're professors doing
real research.

00:53:15.680 --> 00:53:21.690
And so it's an interesting way
of speculating on a big vision

00:53:21.690 --> 00:53:25.260
and what are possible
instruments that lead to that

00:53:25.260 --> 00:53:26.330
big vision.

00:53:26.330 --> 00:53:32.900
Sometimes it's almost like
a marketing campaign.

00:53:32.900 --> 00:53:36.690
It's like putting the idea out
there in order to generate

00:53:36.690 --> 00:53:40.050
enough activity and
discussion.

00:53:40.050 --> 00:53:41.960
And through that,
you might create

00:53:41.960 --> 00:53:44.420
policy change, et cetera.

00:53:44.420 --> 00:53:47.370
In other cases, it's like
speculate on that big vision,

00:53:47.370 --> 00:53:51.610
but what are the intermediary
steps to

00:53:51.610 --> 00:53:53.830
get to that big vision.

00:53:53.830 --> 00:53:59.400
And I think that's the luxury
of being a professor at MIT.

00:53:59.400 --> 00:54:04.150
I think in practice I'm always
thinking of our projects last

00:54:04.150 --> 00:54:06.220
anywhere from three months
to three years.

00:54:06.220 --> 00:54:08.290
We don't really have projects
that last 30

00:54:08.290 --> 00:54:10.490
years in our practice.

00:54:10.490 --> 00:54:12.290
And you're always under
tight deadline.

00:54:12.290 --> 00:54:14.180
And you have to work
with realities.

00:54:14.180 --> 00:54:19.070
And you're doing absolutely the
best under really tight

00:54:19.070 --> 00:54:20.900
time constraints.

00:54:20.900 --> 00:54:25.750
The amazing thing about the
academic environment of MIT is

00:54:25.750 --> 00:54:30.850
they really encourage long- term
thinking and short- term

00:54:30.850 --> 00:54:33.110
projects that get to that
long- term thinking.

00:54:33.110 --> 00:54:35.500
So you can say, I
have this idea.

00:54:35.500 --> 00:54:38.420
I don't think we're there to be
able to implement this in

00:54:38.420 --> 00:54:41.750
the next even 5 to 10 years,
but maybe 20 years

00:54:41.750 --> 00:54:44.210
out, 30 years out.

00:54:44.210 --> 00:54:47.720
And we need to start thinking
about this and talking about

00:54:47.720 --> 00:54:49.790
this now to get there.

00:54:49.790 --> 00:54:50.810
That's the luxury.

00:54:50.810 --> 00:54:54.110
And that's why I can't leave
academia, even though I also

00:54:54.110 --> 00:54:55.830
love practice.

00:54:55.830 --> 00:54:57.400
INTERVIEWER: How do you work
out that balance between--

00:54:57.400 --> 00:54:58.820
you're everywhere all the time?

00:54:58.820 --> 00:54:59.460
YOON: Yes.

00:54:59.460 --> 00:55:01.100
It is an impossible--

00:55:01.100 --> 00:55:05.130
I think there is no such
thing as balance.

00:55:05.130 --> 00:55:08.050
INTERVIEWER: How do your
students respond, MIT students

00:55:08.050 --> 00:55:10.610
in particular, to this laying
out of the big idea.

00:55:10.610 --> 00:55:11.520
Are they thrown by that?

00:55:11.520 --> 00:55:13.360
Do they love it?

00:55:13.360 --> 00:55:14.220
YOON: I think it depends.

00:55:14.220 --> 00:55:16.610
I think like for the graduate
students, I

00:55:16.610 --> 00:55:17.990
think they love it.

00:55:17.990 --> 00:55:19.850
I think the undergraduate
students are

00:55:19.850 --> 00:55:22.160
actually more skeptical.

00:55:22.160 --> 00:55:24.750
Like, no, you need to
teach me how to

00:55:24.750 --> 00:55:28.680
really build real buildings.

00:55:28.680 --> 00:55:33.540
So I think we have both.

00:55:33.540 --> 00:55:35.850
I mean, I think that's what
makes MIT so unique.

00:55:35.850 --> 00:55:38.600
INTERVIEWER: So how do you
introduce them to the joy of

00:55:38.600 --> 00:55:41.790
imagination, free thinking,
letting go of the practical

00:55:41.790 --> 00:55:45.140
for a moment before you
come back to it?

00:55:45.140 --> 00:55:49.590
YOON: Through either abstract
exercises, so abstract that

00:55:49.590 --> 00:55:50.920
they don't really know
they're designing.

00:55:50.920 --> 00:55:54.530
 

00:55:54.530 --> 00:55:57.360
One of my favorite exercises
is I ask

00:55:57.360 --> 00:55:59.240
them to make a drawing.

00:55:59.240 --> 00:56:02.990
But in order for them to make a
drawing, they cannot draw on

00:56:02.990 --> 00:56:04.150
that drawing.

00:56:04.150 --> 00:56:09.540
What they have to do is design
a set of rules, such that

00:56:09.540 --> 00:56:13.960
their classmates each by
contributing one hour of their

00:56:13.960 --> 00:56:18.490
labor on that piece of paper
can produce the drawing.

00:56:18.490 --> 00:56:20.530
And the rules can't
be too elaborate.

00:56:20.530 --> 00:56:23.090
So I make them stick to
one page of rules.

00:56:23.090 --> 00:56:25.970
And it produces the most
extraordinary drawings.

00:56:25.970 --> 00:56:29.740
And this is what I mean by like
thinking through systems

00:56:29.740 --> 00:56:31.400
or designing a system.

00:56:31.400 --> 00:56:37.780
I want the students to design
the system for the drawing.

00:56:37.780 --> 00:56:39.270
They can invent the tool.

00:56:39.270 --> 00:56:43.350
I let them invent the tool
if they want, and

00:56:43.350 --> 00:56:44.750
then a set of rules.

00:56:44.750 --> 00:56:54.180
And then one student says, draw
this specific pattern

00:56:54.180 --> 00:56:56.790
until you hit the edge
and then go this--

00:56:56.790 --> 00:56:59.320
like so it creates
a chain pattern.

00:56:59.320 --> 00:57:06.660
Another student asked them to
put the paper on a wall and

00:57:06.660 --> 00:57:08.570
with their hand try to find--

00:57:08.570 --> 00:57:11.370
because our walls are full of
holes because of all the push

00:57:11.370 --> 00:57:13.740
pins for printing up--

00:57:13.740 --> 00:57:17.830
and copy every location of every
hole, and then the next

00:57:17.830 --> 00:57:20.540
student to connect
all the dots.

00:57:20.540 --> 00:57:23.790
And it produces beautiful
drawings, because they're not

00:57:23.790 --> 00:57:29.160
so willful about some
metaphoric, poetic intent.

00:57:29.160 --> 00:57:36.590
But they are precise and they
are based on a logic that the

00:57:36.590 --> 00:57:38.120
student constructs.

00:57:38.120 --> 00:57:43.710
And what they learn from that
project, I hope, is that

00:57:43.710 --> 00:57:49.200
design is not about pure
individual authorship anymore.

00:57:49.200 --> 00:57:52.420
Design requires the
participation of many.

00:57:52.420 --> 00:57:57.130
But you, as the designer, need
to make sure that there is

00:57:57.130 --> 00:58:01.710
clarity about how that design
will be executed.

00:58:01.710 --> 00:58:04.400
That's one lesson from
that exercise.

00:58:04.400 --> 00:58:07.830
And I think students every
time are just profoundly

00:58:07.830 --> 00:58:11.060
impressed by how beautiful
the drawings are.

00:58:11.060 --> 00:58:14.050
From just an aesthetic point of
view, the drawings always

00:58:14.050 --> 00:58:17.570
end up much more beautiful than
they could have done if

00:58:17.570 --> 00:58:20.200
they sat with a blank
piece of paper and

00:58:20.200 --> 00:58:22.930
tried to draw something.

00:58:22.930 --> 00:58:24.180
INTERVIEWER: That's great.

00:58:24.180 --> 00:58:25.830
That's really good.

00:58:25.830 --> 00:58:26.410
Where do you go from there?

00:58:26.410 --> 00:58:28.320
What's your next exercise?

00:58:28.320 --> 00:58:34.790
YOON: Well, I also do an
exercise where sometimes I ask

00:58:34.790 --> 00:58:40.540
students to take a historic
building, like a '60s and '70s

00:58:40.540 --> 00:58:46.220
building, and we blow up the
photographs really large.

00:58:46.220 --> 00:58:51.540
And I ask them to just sort of
Photoshop, like re-imagine it.

00:58:51.540 --> 00:58:55.700
And so it's not again working
from a blank piece of paper.

00:58:55.700 --> 00:58:59.420
But how would you have
done it differently?

00:58:59.420 --> 00:59:04.000
So one thing they learn is they
have to figure out why

00:59:04.000 --> 00:59:08.310
the original architect
made those decisions.

00:59:08.310 --> 00:59:10.890
Then they come to really
appreciate those decisions.

00:59:10.890 --> 00:59:12.690
Sometimes they're like,
I can't do anything.

00:59:12.690 --> 00:59:15.730
It's really good just
the way it is.

00:59:15.730 --> 00:59:17.050
But I say, okay.

00:59:17.050 --> 00:59:18.930
It was designed as a library.

00:59:18.930 --> 00:59:24.110
Now you have to re-imagine
it as an energy farm.

00:59:24.110 --> 00:59:27.640
And then they kind of loosen
up and they go wild

00:59:27.640 --> 00:59:29.640
and they have fun.

00:59:29.640 --> 00:59:30.730
INTERVIEWER: Is there
something in this

00:59:30.730 --> 00:59:34.360
re-contextualization or
defamiliarizing that is

00:59:34.360 --> 00:59:35.900
involved with your artwork?

00:59:35.900 --> 00:59:38.990
White Noise/White Light or the
Philadelphia piece that drew

00:59:38.990 --> 00:59:40.055
people to the water?

00:59:40.055 --> 00:59:40.380
YOON: Yes.

00:59:40.380 --> 00:59:40.550
No.

00:59:40.550 --> 00:59:42.880
I think there is something I
think that's really important

00:59:42.880 --> 00:59:51.890
in design education that you
need to, for students, break

00:59:51.890 --> 00:59:54.850
down what they think
architecture is.

00:59:54.850 --> 00:59:59.310
Many students when they come
into an architecture program

00:59:59.310 --> 01:00:01.640
they say, oh, I've been around
buildings all my life.

01:00:01.640 --> 01:00:03.760
I know exactly what
a building is.

01:00:03.760 --> 01:00:07.980
Teach me how to draw plans and
I'll go on my merry way and

01:00:07.980 --> 01:00:09.890
design buildings.

01:00:09.890 --> 01:00:13.210
And the first thing is, well,
what is a building?

01:00:13.210 --> 01:00:18.220
What are the things that create
or not create social

01:00:18.220 --> 01:00:19.600
interaction?

01:00:19.600 --> 01:00:21.810
How do you deal with
the ground plane?

01:00:21.810 --> 01:00:24.470
How does something meet
the ground plane?

01:00:24.470 --> 01:00:29.180
How do you think
about barriers?

01:00:29.180 --> 01:00:31.740
From the labyrinth to the
continuity, how can you

01:00:31.740 --> 01:00:34.140
rethink circulation?

01:00:34.140 --> 01:00:38.770
And so we try to abstract those
exercises so students

01:00:38.770 --> 01:00:43.220
really understand space and
not buildings, because our

01:00:43.220 --> 01:00:47.320
goal is to design space, and
that produces buildings.

01:00:47.320 --> 01:00:51.690
A building is just a volume that
separates you from your

01:00:51.690 --> 01:00:53.670
larger environment.

01:00:53.670 --> 01:00:55.520
So what is an envelope?

01:00:55.520 --> 01:00:59.630
How can envelopes perform from
an energy point of view but

01:00:59.630 --> 01:01:02.560
also from a social
point of view.

01:01:02.560 --> 01:01:05.390
Certain buildings, like we were
saying City Hall, it was

01:01:05.390 --> 01:01:07.910
meant to be porous, but it
absolutely doesn't work.

01:01:07.910 --> 01:01:12.020
How can you decide a
porous building?

01:01:12.020 --> 01:01:14.180
INTERVIEWER: So we were talking
about architecture

01:01:14.180 --> 01:01:19.070
moving forward, maybe always,
as a design of space, rather

01:01:19.070 --> 01:01:21.320
than a design of buildings.

01:01:21.320 --> 01:01:23.360
So let's speculate a little
about the future.

01:01:23.360 --> 01:01:28.450
If you had a magic wand and
could redesign the campus,

01:01:28.450 --> 01:01:32.240
take your Photoshop, your real
world Photoshop, and open it

01:01:32.240 --> 01:01:35.900
up and make it more of a
designed, functioning place,

01:01:35.900 --> 01:01:37.150
what would you do?

01:01:37.150 --> 01:01:40.710
 

01:01:40.710 --> 01:01:41.690
YOON: I'd do many things.

01:01:41.690 --> 01:01:46.230
But one of the first schemes I
think I would do, just from

01:01:46.230 --> 01:01:49.050
having been here so long and
understanding the way people

01:01:49.050 --> 01:01:49.920
think and work--

01:01:49.920 --> 01:01:51.570
and it would make a
terrible campus.

01:01:51.570 --> 01:01:55.390
And it would be vetoed by all
architects and planners.

01:01:55.390 --> 01:01:57.790
But I think the MIT community
would love

01:01:57.790 --> 01:02:00.280
to be in one building.

01:02:00.280 --> 01:02:03.900
So it would be one mega-building
that would clear

01:02:03.900 --> 01:02:05.730
across the whole campus.

01:02:05.730 --> 01:02:08.580
There would be lots of
perforations in it for

01:02:08.580 --> 01:02:10.130
courtyards, so there
would be lots of

01:02:10.130 --> 01:02:11.880
day-lighting, et cetera.

01:02:11.880 --> 01:02:15.910
But it would allow you to get
from any department to any

01:02:15.910 --> 01:02:18.110
department without
going outside.

01:02:18.110 --> 01:02:21.300
And it would have lots of--

01:02:21.300 --> 01:02:22.600
I don't know what you
would call it--

01:02:22.600 --> 01:02:27.610
 

01:02:27.610 --> 01:02:32.590
non-defined spaces in terms of
who owns it, so that you could

01:02:32.590 --> 01:02:39.350
have impromptu research
generated by multiple groups

01:02:39.350 --> 01:02:40.520
simultaneously.

01:02:40.520 --> 01:02:42.870
It would be incredibly
transparent.

01:02:42.870 --> 01:02:46.320
It would be all glass on the
interior, so that you could

01:02:46.320 --> 01:02:47.980
see what was going on.

01:02:47.980 --> 01:02:50.800
And it would be no more
than two floors, maybe

01:02:50.800 --> 01:02:52.610
three floors max.

01:02:52.610 --> 01:02:57.440
Which is not a great use of the
land, but what it means is

01:02:57.440 --> 01:02:59.320
everyone would walk.

01:02:59.320 --> 01:03:01.090
So no one would need to
take an elevator and

01:03:01.090 --> 01:03:03.790
go into a dark cube.

01:03:03.790 --> 01:03:07.890
One of the stories I heard once
from a friend of mine who

01:03:07.890 --> 01:03:11.110
used to work at Bell Labs, and
he said it was an amazing

01:03:11.110 --> 01:03:18.080
place to work and that so much
impromptu discussion and

01:03:18.080 --> 01:03:21.750
innovation really occurred
through serendipity, because

01:03:21.750 --> 01:03:24.850
people would run into each other
on the stairs, because

01:03:24.850 --> 01:03:26.705
everyone used the stairs
in Bell Labs.

01:03:26.705 --> 01:03:30.880
And all the great discussions
happened on the landings.

01:03:30.880 --> 01:03:32.530
I thought that was fantastic.

01:03:32.530 --> 01:03:35.230
So if I could redesign MIT,
it would be one giant

01:03:35.230 --> 01:03:38.530
mega-building, a lot of
transparency and day-lighting,

01:03:38.530 --> 01:03:41.080
with millions of courtyards.

01:03:41.080 --> 01:03:44.490
But everything would be two or
three floors so that every one

01:03:44.490 --> 01:03:46.630
would run into each
other on landings.

01:03:46.630 --> 01:03:48.800
And it would be designed in such
a way that no one would

01:03:48.800 --> 01:03:50.110
take one route.

01:03:50.110 --> 01:03:52.510
Maybe it would be designed
so that always the routes

01:03:52.510 --> 01:03:55.640
changed, or you couldn't always
take the same route,

01:03:55.640 --> 01:03:58.250
because I think you get
into a habit at MIT.

01:03:58.250 --> 01:04:00.060
You go down this corridor, this
corridor, this corridor,

01:04:00.060 --> 01:04:00.500
this corridor.

01:04:00.500 --> 01:04:02.670
You run into the same people.

01:04:02.670 --> 01:04:08.700
But if the routes were always
changing in this labyrinth of

01:04:08.700 --> 01:04:12.430
the future MIT, you'd run into
all kinds of people, pass all

01:04:12.430 --> 01:04:14.870
kinds of things, and
be like, huh, wow.

01:04:14.870 --> 01:04:19.480
Someone is creating a robot
that climbs that wall.

01:04:19.480 --> 01:04:21.700
The gecko, that's kind of
interesting, because I'm

01:04:21.700 --> 01:04:26.030
researching a 3M product that
is about sticky surfaces.

01:04:26.030 --> 01:04:29.230
Maybe I should collaborate
with him or talk to him.

01:04:29.230 --> 01:04:30.310
INTERVIEWER: That's very cool.

01:04:30.310 --> 01:04:32.520
I was at Pixar once years
and years ago,

01:04:32.520 --> 01:04:33.760
shortly after they began.

01:04:33.760 --> 01:04:35.850
And I don't know what they're
like now, but at that time

01:04:35.850 --> 01:04:37.420
they were in giant Quonset
huts, kind of

01:04:37.420 --> 01:04:38.480
airplane hangar buildings.

01:04:38.480 --> 01:04:39.460
It was the same deal.

01:04:39.460 --> 01:04:41.540
And people rode around on

01:04:41.540 --> 01:04:45.490
scooters, little hand scooters.

01:04:45.490 --> 01:04:46.600
So that could be
part of it too.

01:04:46.600 --> 01:04:51.920
You could have a take it and
leave it scooter perhaps.

01:04:51.920 --> 01:04:53.840
YOON: That sounds good.

01:04:53.840 --> 01:04:55.020
INTERVIEWER: It's a little
bit like that now.

01:04:55.020 --> 01:04:59.660
I mean, MIT, it's embryonic and
not fully realized, but it

01:04:59.660 --> 01:05:02.160
does seem to be that there's
a lot of meeting in the

01:05:02.160 --> 01:05:03.850
hallways, meeting
in corridors.

01:05:03.850 --> 01:05:04.270
YOON: Yes.

01:05:04.270 --> 01:05:04.600
Yes.

01:05:04.600 --> 01:05:10.230
But I feel like the spaces are
not the kind of spaces you

01:05:10.230 --> 01:05:13.500
want to just linger in
forever and ever.

01:05:13.500 --> 01:05:19.490
It is just the result of
corridors and hallways and

01:05:19.490 --> 01:05:24.190
bridges and connections, as
opposed to being really

01:05:24.190 --> 01:05:29.150
thought of as network.

01:05:29.150 --> 01:05:32.030
INTERVIEWER: This comes back
to the FAST Festival, where

01:05:32.030 --> 01:05:36.250
the pictures I saw-- it was
not here, but the Green

01:05:36.250 --> 01:05:39.160
Building and that area, it did
seem to make it into a

01:05:39.160 --> 01:05:44.220
networked community public space
in a way that, to me at

01:05:44.220 --> 01:05:45.470
least, it just feels--

01:05:45.470 --> 01:05:46.340
YOON: Isn't now.

01:05:46.340 --> 01:05:46.600
INTERVIEWER: Yes.

01:05:46.600 --> 01:05:47.770
It's not now.

01:05:47.770 --> 01:05:50.650
Was that a deliberate aim that
the students had, or you had?

01:05:50.650 --> 01:05:53.750
YOON: Well, you know, yes.

01:05:53.750 --> 01:05:57.550
I mean, I think that, for
me, for the windscreen

01:05:57.550 --> 01:06:01.340
installation I did on the Green
Building, for me that

01:06:01.340 --> 01:06:06.640
place on the campus is
strange, because

01:06:06.640 --> 01:06:07.990
first it's a tower.

01:06:07.990 --> 01:06:10.840
But also because it's so
windy nobody enjoys

01:06:10.840 --> 01:06:14.150
being in that space.

01:06:14.150 --> 01:06:18.900
And even though the other side
of the Green Building runs

01:06:18.900 --> 01:06:24.470
parallel to the extension of the
Infinite Corridor, because

01:06:24.470 --> 01:06:27.000
the weather's always so
bad nobody uses it.

01:06:27.000 --> 01:06:32.620
So I do think the goal was to
actually take spaces that are

01:06:32.620 --> 01:06:37.310
underutilized on MIT's
campus and then

01:06:37.310 --> 01:06:38.690
make them more utilized.

01:06:38.690 --> 01:06:44.820
Or to use some aspect
of them to--

01:06:44.820 --> 01:06:46.196
INTERVIEWER: So what are
you working on now?

01:06:46.196 --> 01:06:47.830
What's the next big
art project or

01:06:47.830 --> 01:06:48.730
architecture project?

01:06:48.730 --> 01:06:52.840
YOON: We're doing a couple
interesting projects.

01:06:52.840 --> 01:06:58.340
We were asked recently to do a
permanent installation art

01:06:58.340 --> 01:07:02.390
piece as part of the General
Service Administration's

01:07:02.390 --> 01:07:05.400
Percent for Art and Architecture
program.

01:07:05.400 --> 01:07:08.460
So it's fun, because I get to
change hats and be the artist

01:07:08.460 --> 01:07:10.600
in an architecture project.

01:07:10.600 --> 01:07:13.820
But this is for the San Ysidro
border crossing, which is the

01:07:13.820 --> 01:07:17.650
largest land border crossing
in the world.

01:07:17.650 --> 01:07:19.240
And so they're expanding it.

01:07:19.240 --> 01:07:21.155
It'll be 34 lanes of traffic.

01:07:21.155 --> 01:07:25.830
They process 3,000 people an
hour in the pedestrian lane.

01:07:25.830 --> 01:07:27.070
INTERVIEWER: California-Mexico?

01:07:27.070 --> 01:07:27.590
YOON: Yes.

01:07:27.590 --> 01:07:28.590
Yes.

01:07:28.590 --> 01:07:30.430
INTERVIEWER: 3,000
people an hour?

01:07:30.430 --> 01:07:31.480
YOON: That doesn't seem
right, does it?

01:07:31.480 --> 01:07:33.890
It must be 3,000--

01:07:33.890 --> 01:07:37.240
3,000 people a day doesn't
sound right either.

01:07:37.240 --> 01:07:37.545
INTERVIEWER: 3,000 an hour.

01:07:37.545 --> 01:07:37.850
Could be.

01:07:37.850 --> 01:07:38.940
YOON: Yes.

01:07:38.940 --> 01:07:46.850
And we're doing a solar-powered
canopy in the

01:07:46.850 --> 01:07:51.370
city of Phoenix on Roosevelt
Row, was another project.

01:07:51.370 --> 01:07:55.240
On the architectural front,
we're looking at redeveloping

01:07:55.240 --> 01:07:58.300
Avery and Hayward Street here
in Boston, the streetscape.

01:07:58.300 --> 01:08:02.500
 

01:08:02.500 --> 01:08:04.490
What else are we doing?

01:08:04.490 --> 01:08:05.240
We have a lot of projects.

01:08:05.240 --> 01:08:08.370
Oh, we're doing the
plaza in front of

01:08:08.370 --> 01:08:10.330
Harvard Science Center.

01:08:10.330 --> 01:08:14.770
We're working with Stoss, which
is a landscape firm, to

01:08:14.770 --> 01:08:15.520
rethink that.

01:08:15.520 --> 01:08:19.750
And we're specifically focusing
on the media aspects,

01:08:19.750 --> 01:08:23.125
lighting and media aspects
of that project.

01:08:23.125 --> 01:08:25.890
 

01:08:25.890 --> 01:08:28.640
INTERVIEWER: So people say you
should never go into business

01:08:28.640 --> 01:08:30.380
with your friends.

01:08:30.380 --> 01:08:33.160
You're in business with
your husband.

01:08:33.160 --> 01:08:35.569
How does that work out?

01:08:35.569 --> 01:08:36.819
YOON: We're still friends.

01:08:36.819 --> 01:08:38.630
 

01:08:38.630 --> 01:08:45.189
So my husband was one
year ahead of me in

01:08:45.189 --> 01:08:47.240
undergrad at Cornell.

01:08:47.240 --> 01:08:50.090
So he was an upperclassman
by one year.

01:08:50.090 --> 01:08:54.899
And when we graduated, he went
to New York and then I went to

01:08:54.899 --> 01:09:01.340
Boston for my Master's, or
Cambridge for my Master's.

01:09:01.340 --> 01:09:04.069
And he continued on his track.

01:09:04.069 --> 01:09:08.930
So he worked for eight years at
KPF, big firm in New York.

01:09:08.930 --> 01:09:12.870
And then three years for Diller
Scofidio, which is the

01:09:12.870 --> 01:09:17.260
boutique firm that the ICA
project here in Boston.

01:09:17.260 --> 01:09:18.680
And I did my thing.

01:09:18.680 --> 01:09:22.130
I did my public space
installations.

01:09:22.130 --> 01:09:25.859
I did small- scale residential
projects.

01:09:25.859 --> 01:09:27.189
And in--

01:09:27.189 --> 01:09:27.850
I don't remember--

01:09:27.850 --> 01:09:31.910
2006 we said we'd join forces.

01:09:31.910 --> 01:09:34.050
And we were like, what kind of
practice are we going to have?

01:09:34.050 --> 01:09:36.240
Because he has big--

01:09:36.240 --> 01:09:38.149
he did towers at KPF.

01:09:38.149 --> 01:09:41.250
He worked on the tallest
buildings in the world.

01:09:41.250 --> 01:09:45.000
And Diller Scofidio, he worked
on really important cultural

01:09:45.000 --> 01:09:46.829
venues, like the Institute
of Contemporary

01:09:46.829 --> 01:09:50.250
Art and Lincoln Center.

01:09:50.250 --> 01:09:54.340
And me, I had done an
installation at the

01:09:54.340 --> 01:09:59.410
Guggenheim, and for the Athens
Olympics, and much smaller-

01:09:59.410 --> 01:10:01.265
scale interventions.

01:10:01.265 --> 01:10:03.130
A dress.

01:10:03.130 --> 01:10:05.910
What kind of firm are
we going to have?

01:10:05.910 --> 01:10:10.740
So we've combined our forces
and it's been an adventure.

01:10:10.740 --> 01:10:16.890
I mean, I think we both really
rely on each other's opinion.

01:10:16.890 --> 01:10:19.160
We really respect it.

01:10:19.160 --> 01:10:23.510
But we have very different
approaches to design, so it

01:10:23.510 --> 01:10:25.680
works out well.

01:10:25.680 --> 01:10:27.620
INTERVIEWER: In a way, you're
kind of embodying this future

01:10:27.620 --> 01:10:29.640
for the field that you're
talking about, combining

01:10:29.640 --> 01:10:33.700
designing space and art
influences with a more

01:10:33.700 --> 01:10:35.980
traditional building approach.

01:10:35.980 --> 01:10:36.360
YOON: Yes.

01:10:36.360 --> 01:10:36.910
Yes.

01:10:36.910 --> 01:10:39.140
INTERVIEWER: So you represent
the future.

01:10:39.140 --> 01:10:42.276
So I'll come back in another
20 years and

01:10:42.276 --> 01:10:42.540
see where you are.

01:10:42.540 --> 01:10:46.780
YOON: Hopefully, we'll still
be in operation.

01:10:46.780 --> 01:10:49.386
INTERVIEWER: So why
do you teach?

01:10:49.386 --> 01:10:52.314
 

01:10:52.314 --> 01:10:52.810
You don't have to.

01:10:52.810 --> 01:10:54.730
YOON: Yes.

01:10:54.730 --> 01:10:55.450
I don't know.

01:10:55.450 --> 01:10:56.920
I can't not teach.

01:10:56.920 --> 01:11:02.065
I mean, I feel always inspired
by teaching.

01:11:02.065 --> 01:11:04.780
 

01:11:04.780 --> 01:11:08.360
Like I'm on sabbatical this
semester, so I've been really

01:11:08.360 --> 01:11:12.770
focusing on the work in the
studio, in the office.

01:11:12.770 --> 01:11:14.030
And I miss teaching.

01:11:14.030 --> 01:11:21.330
Like I have this itch to be
around young, energetic people

01:11:21.330 --> 01:11:25.620
who are unencumbered by the
realities of the world.

01:11:25.620 --> 01:11:36.560
And they encourage me to always
think with open eyes,

01:11:36.560 --> 01:11:40.880
with an open mind
about a problem.

01:11:40.880 --> 01:11:46.720
And because I'm professing a
certain way I think that

01:11:46.720 --> 01:11:54.350
design should be thought about,
as I profess to them, I

01:11:54.350 --> 01:11:55.510
can't be a hypocrite.

01:11:55.510 --> 01:12:01.210
So it keeps our office, I think,
and studio having the

01:12:01.210 --> 01:12:06.930
highest ambitions in terms of
design and not giving in and

01:12:06.930 --> 01:12:12.258
kind of selling out to the
realities of the world.

01:12:12.258 --> 01:12:13.470
INTERVIEWER: They keep
you on your toes.

01:12:13.470 --> 01:12:14.870
YOON: They keep me on my toes.

01:12:14.870 --> 01:12:17.320
Yes.

01:12:17.320 --> 01:12:18.570
INTERVIEWER: Well,
that's great.

01:12:18.570 --> 01:12:22.030
 

01:12:22.030 --> 01:12:22.810
I think that's all I have.

01:12:22.810 --> 01:12:23.960
Thank you so much.

01:12:23.960 --> 01:12:24.710
YOON: Thank you.

01:12:24.710 --> 01:12:25.960
Thank you so much.

01:12:25.960 --> 01:12:29.249
 

